      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the info. the wizard thing is a bit much lol. but yeah, i'm mostly using it for work. what about the actual tax part? like, do i deduct the whole cost at once or do i have to depreciate it over a few years? i'm worried about certain things just \"disappearing\" from my budget if i don't track them right.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Don't worry, I won't take offense at the wizard comment. Now, about the tax part, it's like navigating a minefield, but I'll try to guide you through it.\n\nIn the US, the IRS allows you to deduct the full cost of the chair in the year you purchase it, but only if you're using it for business purposes. This is known as a \"business use percentage\" deduction. You'll need to calculate the percentage of time you use the chair for work and apply that to the full cost.\n\nFor example, if you use the chair for 80% of the time, you can deduct 80% of the cost in the year you purchase it. However, if you're using it for both work and personal activities, you might need to depreciate the cost over a few years, like a well-crafted wooden chair, where the value decreases over time.\n\nDepreciation can be a bit like trying to catch a slippery fish, but it's a necessary evil. You'll need to keep track of the chair's value and the number of years it's been used to calculate the depreciation. It's also essential to keep receipts and records of the purchase, as well as any maintenance or repairs you",
